Title: SIGPIPE-class grep|grep pipe in test_harness_rules_injection.sh:44
Created: 2026-07-14
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-07-14T12:40Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

Gaze review of PR #584 (ticket 0329) flagged a pre-existing pipeline at
`tests/test_harness_rules_injection.sh:44`:

```bash
if ! grep -F 'cat' scripts/on-start.sh | grep -qF 'README.md'; then
```

Under `set -o pipefail`, the trailing `grep -q` exits on first match and can
close the pipe before the upstream `grep -F` finishes writing; the upstream
then dies with SIGPIPE (141), which pipefail reports as the pipeline status —
a nondeterministic false FAIL. This is the same defect class fixed suite-wide
in PR #575 (72659a1, printf|grep) and PR #584 (grep-here-string), but this
site greps a FILE through a filter pipe, so it was out of scope for both.

PR #584 left it untouched deliberately (different mechanism than the 0329
here-string flake); this ticket tracks the residual site.

## Relevant files
- `tests/test_harness_rules_injection.sh` — line 44, the `grep | grep -q` pipe

## Actions
1. Replace the pipe with a form that cannot SIGPIPE, e.g. capture then match:
   `line=$(grep -F 'cat' scripts/on-start.sh || true)` then
   `[[ "$line" == *README.md* ]]`; or drop the first stage and match the
   README.md needle on the cat-line directly with a single grep against the file.
2. Sweep the same suite for any sibling `grep | grep -q` pipes.

## Test
- Suite `tests/test_harness_rules_injection.sh` stays green standalone and
  under `make check`; the converted check still fails loud when the needle is
  removed from `scripts/on-start.sh` (throwaway-copy mutation).

## Invariants
- Fail-loud semantics preserved; no retry or `|| true` on the assertion itself.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] No `grep | grep -q` pipeline remains in tests/test_harness_rules_injection.sh
- [ ] Mutation check confirms the converted site still fails on needle removal
- [ ] `make check` passes
